  "worked_solution": "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nLet $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}].$ If $k\\ne 1$, then\n\n$$\ndet(A)=det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}]=1-k\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nSo, according to the invertible matrix theorem $A$ is invertible and $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ is the unique solution of  $Ax=0.$\nAlso, the system \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \nhas exactly one solution, and the lines $x+y=0$ and $x+ky=0$ intersect at exactly one point. \n\nTherefore, the true statement is as follows:\n\n*If $k\\ne 1$, then the system $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has the unique zero solution.* $\\,\u2713$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
